age:47\"}],\"requested_count\":3,\"queued_count\":8}},{\"tool\":\"recall\",\"arguments\":{},\"result\":{\"requested\":[{\"id\":\"ev-f03e4b7f1b7fc6e66886\",\"source\":\"paper.pdf\",\"locator\":\"page:47\"},{\"id\":\"ev-aa9b9dbdfec8c640b495\",\"source\":\"paper.pdf\",\"locator\":\"page:48\"},{\"id\":\"ev-6e64db9ec6bfa8f9417f\",\"source\":\"paper.pdf\",\"locator\":\"page:49\"}],\"queued\":[{\"id\":\"ev-f03e4b7f1b7fc6e66886\",\"source\":\"paper.pdf\",\"locator\":\"page:47\"},{\"id\":\"ev-aa9b9dbdfec8c640b495\",\"source\":\"paper.pdf\",\"locator\":\"page:48\"},{\"id\":\"ev-6e64db9ec6bfa8f9417f\",\"source\":\"paper.pdf\",\"locator\":\"page:49\"}],\"requested_count\":4,\"queued_count\":6}},{\"tool\":\"write_note\",\"arguments\":{},\"result\":{\"note_id\":\"multi_agent\",\"sources\":[\"paper.pdf\"],\"chars\":1027}},{\"tool\":\"recall\",\"arguments\":{},\"result\":{\"requested\":[{\"id\":\"ev-99f0f0a37d328bfed1a9\",\"source\":\"paper.pdf\",\"locator\":\"page:51\"},{\"id\":\"ev-01ce8948bcc88d235ea3\",\"source\":\"paper.pdf\",\"locator\":\"page:52\"}],\"queued\":[{\"id\":\"ev-99f0f0a37d328bfed1a9\",\"source\":\"paper.pdf\",\"locator\":\"page:51\"},{\"id\":\"ev-01ce8948bcc88d235ea3\",\"source\":\"paper.pdf\",\"locator\":\"page:52\"}],\"requested_count\":2,\"queued_count\":2}}]"}], "tool_calls": null, "raw": null, "token_usage": null}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Model-authored note overview, sources=['paper.pdf']:\nOverview notes (pp.1-6). Agentic LLMs are defined as agents that receive natural-language input, reason, act and interact; the survey is organized by the three categories reason / act / interact (categories answer). The three build on each other: reasoning technology enables acting by assistants, acting/interaction enables social simulation, and outcomes feed data augmentation to finetune LLMs (virtuous circle, Fig.1, p.4). Literature selection (Sec.1.2, p.5): 'The papers were initially selected with a Google Scholar search on Agentic LLM. From there, we used a snowballing approach to discover work that was cited but not yet included in our initial set.' So search_methods = google_scholar, snowballing. Only LLM-based approaches kept; most works 2024, some 2023/2025. Sec.1.3 training pipeline (pretrain, SFT, instruction tuning, alignment RLHF/DPO/RLVR, in-context learning); Sec.1.4 motivates agentic LLMs with four challenges: prompt engineering, hallucination, ..."}], "tool_calls": null, "raw": null, "token_usage": null}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Model-authored note reasoning, sources=['paper.pdf']:\nReasoning notes (final). Self-Consistency (Sec.2.1.1, p.10): 'Self Consistency is an ensemble approach that samples diverse reasoning paths, evaluates them, and selects the most consistent answer using majority voting.' So self_consistency_selection = majority voting over sampled reasoning paths' answers; it lifts CoT by 10-20 points and underpins o1/o3/DeepSeek/Qwen. Tree of Thoughts (Sec.2.1.3, p.13): 'The paper describes both a breadth-first and a depth-first controller.' So tot_search = breadth_first, depth_first; ToT adds an external search controller with generation + evaluation prompts and backtracking. Graph of Thoughts generalizes to graph-structured thoughts. CoT alone is greedy single-path; Self-Consistency/ToT build on CoT."}], "tool_calls": null, "raw": null, "token_usage": null}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Model-authored note tool_use, sources=['paper.pdf']:\nTool-use notes (p.21-23). Acting via tools: Toolformer (Schick et al.) is trained to decide which APIs to call, when, with what arguments, and how to use the results (p.23). Tested tools: 'A range of tools is tested: a calculator, a question-answering system, a search engine, a translation system, and a calendar.' so toolformer_tools = calculator, question_answering, search_engine, translation, calendar. ToolBench: 'ToolBench [Qin et al., 2023] contains 16,464 APIs from RapidAPI' -> toolbench_api_count = 16464. Related: EasyTool builds structured tool instructions on ToolBench; ToolAlpaca (3938 instances, 400 APIs); Gorilla/APIBench; planner-caller-summarizer framework with three LLMs (Fig.13). Pages 21-22 cover embodied/VLA robots, SayCan, Inner Monologue, zero-shot object navigation."}], "tool_calls": null, "raw": null, "token_usage": null}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Model-authored note multi_agent, sources=['paper.pdf']:\nMulti-agent notes (final, pp.36-37,47). CAMEL (Sec.4.2.2, p.37): two LLMs take predefined roles and 'cooperatively drive a conversation without continuous human prompting. By using inception prompting and role descriptions, the agents stay in character and collaborate toward the goal by breaking down complex problems in manageable steps through dialogue.' So camel_prompting = inception_prompting. Generative Agents (p.47): 'Whether the Generative Agents system generates believable behavior is evaluated with the help of 100 human evaluators.' They ranked believability of communication patterns in four categories (remember, plan, react, reflect) across four architectures; the full architecture performed best, though erratic behavior and hallucination appeared. Also covered: creator-critic pairs, Constitutional AI, multi-agent debate, MAD with a judge, round-table confidence-weighted voting, AutoGen, ChatEval, Sotopia, GovSim. Related 'Simulating 1000 People' recruited 1052 humans for interview-based agent profiles."}], "tool_calls": null, "raw": null, "token_usage": null}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Evidence ev-99f0f0a37d328bfed1a9 | paper.pdf | page:51 | sha256=b4767423c79db69ae7138f4c0aed7733af6385e0a370da21ad7d6fe029d7fb8c\n                                                                                                               Agentic LLM     •   1:51\n\n\n         Topic               Challenge\n         Training Data       Finetune with inference time reasoning data\n                             Convergent/stable reinforcement learning\n                             VLA, Multimodal signals, such as speech\n         Hallucination       Use Self Verification\n                             Use Mechanistic Interpretability\n                             Use Open Ended/Open World Models for exploration\n         Agent Behavior      Scalable simulation infrastructure, role playing\n                             Distill reasoning to small models\n                             Models of agent and human behavior, emergent behavior, future of society\n         Self reflection     In-model self reflection and metareasoning\n                             Metacognition, personality\n                             Automated Scientific Discovery\n         Safety              Assistants: Responsibility, liability\n                             Privacy, fairness of data\n                             Wider application areas for assistants\n                                Table 4. Summary of Research Agenda for Agentic LLM\n\n\n\n\n  When we add outside state to the input prompts, the input to the LLM will differ based on the history, and so\nwill the answers of the LLM. Differences in memory may be preceived as a personality of the LLMs by its users.\nThe question if LLMs with outside memory exhibit a personality is a topic for future research.\n  Self reflective methods are being used to create agents to perform scientific discovery [Eger et al., 2025]. How\nthese agents will influence, and possibly improve, the process of scientific discovery is an exciting area of research.\n\n   Safety. Safety is a crucial issue in LLMs that act in the world. The problem is studied, but far from being solved\n[Brunke et al., 2022, Andriushchenko et al., 2025, Samvelyan et al., 2024]. Actions by assistants and robots in the\nreal world have real world consequences. When a financial trading assistant hallucinates, or when a self driving\nrobot makes a wrong inference, questions on responsibility and liability should be addressed. More legal and\nethical questions arise, for example, on privacy and fairness, and, possibly, concerning the rights of algorithmic\nentities [Harris and Anthis, 2021, Bengio and Elmoznino, 2025].\n   The application areas for the assistants in this survey—shopping, medical diagnosis, finance—are narrow. The\nnarrower the application domain, the better the answers.\n   Ensuring the safety of agentic LLMs requires moving beyond prompt-based defenses toward integrated, multi-\nlayer safeguards. Key directions include embedding explicit safety constraints within the agent’s reasoning\nand planning pipeline and employing continual adversarial training and automated red-teaming to enhance\nrobustness against manipulation. Further priorities are developing mechanisms for self-regulation and risk\nawareness, enabling agents to detect and avoid unsafe actions, and establishing rigorous, standardized safety\nbenchmarks such as AgentHarm [Andriushchenko et al., 2025]. Together, these measures outline a roadmap for\naccountable and trustworthy deployment of agentic LLMs in high-stakes domains.\n   Clearly, many safety, ethics and trust issues will have to be addressed before the full breadth of the possibilities\nof agentic LLMs can be enjoyed. Safety and ethics will become important topics on the research agenda of agentic\nLLMs, deserving their own surveys and books [Gan et al., 2024, Jiao et al., 2025, Raza et al., 2025].\n\n                                       Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\n"}], "tool_calls": null, "raw": null, "token_usage": null}, {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Evidence ev-01ce8948bcc88d235ea3 | paper.pdf | page:52 | sha256=b4767423c79db69ae7138f4c0aed7733af6385e0a370da21ad7d6fe029d7fb8c\n1:52   •    Plaat, Van Duijn, Van Stein, Preuss, Van der Putten & Batenburg\n\n\n5.2        Conclusion\nThere is a large research activity on agentic LLMs. Already, robots show impressive generalization results, and so\ndo assistants in medical diagnosis, financial market advising, and scientific research. Work processes in these—and\nother—fields may well be affected by agentic LLM assistants in the near future.\n   The agentic LLMs in this survey have (1) reasoning capabilities, (2) an interface to the outside world in order\nto act, and (3) a social environment with other agents with which to interact. The categories of this taxonomy\ncomplement each other. At the basis is the reasoning technology of category 1. Robotic interaction and tool-use\nbuild on grounded retrieval augmentation, social interaction (such as theory of mind) builds on self reflection,\nand all categories benefit from reasoning and self-verification. Closing the cycle, the acting and interacting\ncategories generate training data for further pretraining and finetuning LLMs, beyond plateauing traditional\ndatasets (Figure 1). The impressive generalization capabilities of Vision-Language-Action models are testament\nto the power of this approach.\n   The reasoning paradigm connects to works in human cognition, and some papers anthropomorphize LLM\ncomputations in Kahneman’s terms of System 1 thinking (fast, associative) and System 2 thinking (slow, delibera-\ntive). Works on reasoning focus on the intelligence of single LLMs. This individualistic view also gives rise to\ndiscussions about superintelligence, some utopian, some not.\n   The agentic paradigm enables two elements of machine learning that are new for LLMs. First, in reinforcement\nlearning, agents self reflect and choose their own actions, and learn from the feedback of the world in which they\noperate. Second, no dataset is needed, nor is learning limited by the complexity of a dataset, it is only limited by\nthe complexity of the world around the agent. The agent paradigm creates a more challenging training setting,\nallowing agentic LLMs to keep improving themselves.\n   The multi-agent paradigm studies agent-agent societies. The focus is on emergent behaviors such as ego-\nism/altruism, competition/collaboration, and (dis)trust. Social cognitive development and the emergence of\ncollective intelligence are also studied in this field. Connecting back to the reasoning paradigm, the collaboratieve\nview of multi-agent studies may inform discussions about (super)intelligence, teaching us about emerging social\nbehavior of LLM-agents.\n\nAcknowledgements\nWe thank Joost Broekens, Suzan Verberne, Annie Wong, Thomas Bäck, Zhaochun Ren, Thomas Moerland, Michiel\nvan der Meer, Bram Renting, Frank van Harmelen, Tessa Verhoef, and Rob van Nieuwpoort for extensive and\nfruitful discussions. We thank the anonymous reviewers for valuable suggestions that have improved the article.\n\nReferences\nJosh Achiam, Steven Adler, Sandhini Agarwal, Lama Ahmad, Ilge Akkaya, Florencia Leoni Aleman, Diogo\n  Almeida, Janko Altenschmidt, Sam Altman, Shyamal Anadkat, et al. GPT-4 technical report. arXiv preprint\n  arXiv:2303.08774, 2023.\nConstructions Aeronautiques, Adele Howe, Craig Knoblock, ISI Drew McDermott, Ashwin Ram, Manuela Veloso,\n  Daniel Weld, David Wilkins Sri, Anthony Barrett, Dave Christianson, et al. PDDL – the planning domain\n  definition language. Technical Report, Tech. Rep., 1998.\nBlaise Agüera y Arcas. What Is Intelligence?: Lessons from AI About Evolution, Computing, and Minds. MIT Press,\n  2025.\nMichael Ahn, Anthony Brohan, Yevgen Chebotar, Chelsea Finn, Karol Hausman, Alexander Herzog, Daniel\n  Ho, Julian Ibarz, Alex Irpan, Eric Jang, Ryan Julian, Dmitry Kalashnikov, Sergey Levine, Yao Lu, Carolina\n  Parada, Kanishka Rao, Pierre Sermanet, Alexander T Toshev, Vincent Vanhoucke, Fei Xia, Ted Xiao, Peng Xu,\n  Mengyuan Yan, Noah Brown, Michael Ahn, Omar Cortes, Nicolas Sievers, Clayton Tan, Sichun Xu, Diego\n\nJournal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\n"}], "tool_calls": null, "raw": null, "token_usage": null}], "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_25", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "python_interpreter", "arguments": "r = write_note(title='outlook', content=\"Outlook notes (pp.51-52). Table 4 (p.51) lists five research agenda topics with challenges: 'Training Data' (finetune with inference-time reasoning data, convergent/stable RL, VLA/multimodal speech), 'Hallucination' (self verification, mechanistic interpretability, open-ended/open-world models), 'Agent Behavior' (scalable simulation infrastructure and role playing, distill reasoning to small models, models of agent/human behavior and emergent behavior), 'Self reflection' (in-model self reflection and metareasoning, metacognition/personality, automated scientific discovery) and 'Safety' (responsibility/liability, privacy/fairness, wider application areas). So agenda_topics = training_data, hallucination, agent_behavior, self_reflection, safety. Conclusion (Sec.5.2, p.52): 'Robotic interaction and tool-use build on grounded retrieval augmentation, social interaction (such as theory of mind) builds on self reflection, and all categories benefit from reasoning and self-verification.' So shared_foundations = reasoning, self_verification. Safety (p.51) calls for multi-layer safeguards, continual adversarial training and red-teaming, self-regulation, and benchmarks such as AgentHarm.\", evidence_ids=['ev-99f0f0a37d328bfed1a9','ev-01ce8948bcc88d235ea3'], section='outlook')\nprint(r)\nrep = read_file('report.json')\nprint(rep['file_hash'], repr(rep['content'])[:300])"}}], "error": {"type": "AgentExecutionError", "message": "Code execution failed at line 'print(rep['file_hash'], repr(rep['content'])[:300])' due to: InterpreterError: Forbidden function evaluation: 'repr' is not among the explicitly allowed tools or defined/imported in the preceding code"}, "model_output_message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Table 4 and the conclusion are now avai