 "object": "chat.completion", "moderation": null, "service_tier": null, "system_fingerprint": "bc19ba97cbe016479903222bb1578db6", "usage": {"_type": "CompletionUsage", "completion_tokens": 901, "prompt_tokens": 7730, "total_tokens": 8631, "completion_tokens_details": {"_type": "CompletionTokensDetails", "accepted_prediction_tokens": null, "audio_tokens": null, "reasoning_tokens": 755, "rejected_prediction_tokens": null}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"_type": "PromptTokensDetails", "audio_tokens": null, "cache_write_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 4352}}, "_request_id": null}, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 7730, "output_tokens": 901, "total_tokens": 8631}}, "model_output": "Extracting exact quotations for the overview answers from pages 1–6 (only snippets printed).\n\n```python\nr = run_command([\"python\",\"read_pdf.py\",\"--start-page\",\"1\",\"--end-page\",\"6\"])\ndata = json.loads(r['stdout'])\nfor p in data['pages']:\n    t = p['text']\n    for kw in ['Google Scholar','snowball','reason, act','three categories','Reason, Act']:\n        i = t.find(kw)\n        if i >= 0:\n            print('PAGE', p['page'], 'KW', kw)\n            print(t[max(0,i-500):i+350])\n            print('---')\n```", "code_action": "r = run_command([\"python\",\"read_pdf.py\",\"--start-page\",\"1\",\"--end-page\",\"6\"])\ndata = json.loads(r['stdout'])\nfor p in data['pages']:\n    t = p['text']\n    for kw in ['Google Scholar','snowball','reason, act','three categories','Reason, Act']:\n        i = t.find(kw)\n        if i >= 0:\n            print('PAGE', p['page'], 'KW', kw)\n            print(t[max(0,i-500):i+350])\n            print('---')", "observations": "Execution logs:\nPAGE 3 KW reason, act\nsuch as cooperative problem-solving, social coordination\n        and norms.\n      • We show how the three categories—reasoning–acting–interacting—complement each other, and how they\n        help to generate additional data for pretraining, finetuning, and augmenting inference time behavior, as\n        shown in Figure 1.\n      • We formulate a research agenda with promising directions for future work (Section 5, Table 4).\n\n1.1     Agentic LLMs: Reasoning–Acting–Interacting\nModels predict, agents reason, act, and interact. To do so, they must have the ability to find new information,\nreflect, make decisions, and communicate. Additionally, where models are passive in the sense that they provide\noutput only in response to specific input, agents have a degree of autonomy. From the fields of natural language\nprocessing, robotics, reinforcement \n---\nPAGE 3 KW three categories\n                                                                                                                       Agentic LLM     •   1:3\n\n\n\n\nFig. 1. Virtuous Cycle connecting the three categories of the Agentic LLM taxonomy: reasoning, acting, and interacting\n(in red, corresponding to Sections 2, 3, and 4). Concepts that influence a category are in green (Subsections). Feedback\nloops, where reasoning, acting, and interacting generate new data for pretraining and finetuning LLMs, are also indicated.\n(Feedback loops may desta\n---\nPAGE 5 KW Google Scholar\nbolic AI, robotics/autonomous systems, and connectionism/multi-agent modeling,\nrespectively. Agentic LLMs are thus both a recent development and build on decades of research. This is reflected\nin our discussion below.\n\n1.2   Literature Selection\nThe field of agentic LLM is rich and active. This survey can only cover the current status of the field. We\nhope to provide clarity about the main approaches, to ease the entry of new researchers into the field. The\npapers were initially selected with a Google Scholar search on Agentic LLM. From there, we used a snowballing\napproach to discover work that was cited but not yet included in our initial set. We have only selected LLM-based\napproaches, excluding multi-agent work without LLMs. In addition, some works on LLMs that do not involve\nagentic augmentations are included to provide background.\n \n---\nPAGE 5 KW snowball\nti-agent modeling,\nrespectively. Agentic LLMs are thus both a recent development and build on decades of research. This is reflected\nin our discussion below.\n\n1.2   Literature Selection\nThe field of agentic LLM is rich and active. This survey can only cover the current status of the field. We\nhope to provide clarity about the main approaches, to ease the entry of new researchers into the field. The\npapers were initially selected with a Google Scholar search on Agentic LLM. From there, we used a snowballing\napproach to discover work that was cited but not yet included in our initial set. We have only selected LLM-based\napproaches, excluding multi-agent work without LLMs. In addition, some works on LLMs that do not involve\nagentic augmentations are included to provide background.\n   Related surveys on agents and LLMs are starting to appear.\n---\nLast output from code snippet:\n-1", "observations_images": null, "action_output": -1, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 7730, "output_tokens": 901, "total_tokens": 8631}, "is_final_answer": false}}
